<!-- Website-only teaching for android/performance/battery-power-profiling.md; original chapter remains read-only. Sources checked 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->

<p class="story-lead">The app is idle on screen, yet the battery chart keeps moving. A retry loop, location session or wake lock may be keeping hardware busy. Start by identifying the work that continues after useful activity ends.</p>

Define one scenario and compare like-for-like device conditions before attributing power to a feature.

<!-- @after What Drains Battery :: visual -->

### Work, hardware activity, useful outcome

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:3"><div class="node"><strong>Work requested</strong><small>Jobs, location updates, retries and rendering.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Hardware activity</strong><small>CPU, radio, sensors, display and associated residency.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Product value</strong><small>Did the work deliver the required freshness or user action?</small></div></div><figcaption>Moving work off Main can improve responsiveness without reducing the amount of work. A power claim needs power evidence.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Profiling Protocol :: animation -->

### Isolate a power hypothesis

<figure class="stepper"><span class="stepper-title">Isolate a power hypothesis</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="scenario"><strong>Scenario</strong><small>Idle after login</small></div><div class="node" data-el="activity"><strong>Activity</strong><small>Unexpected repeated work</small></div><div class="node" data-el="change"><strong>Change</strong><small>One bounded policy</small></div><div class="node" data-el="compare"><strong>Comparison</strong><small>Same duration and conditions</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Define", "caption": "Specify duration, app state, network, device and useful outcome. Check which power signals the device/tool actually supports.", "state": {"scenario": "active"}, "add": ["scenario", "activity", "change", "compare"]}, {"label": "Correlate", "caption": "Look for work and hardware activity during the interval. Timing correlation suggests a hypothesis; it does not prove per-app energy attribution.", "state": {"scenario": "done", "activity": "active"}}, {"label": "Constrain", "caption": "For this illustrative retry case, bound attempts and preserve required durable work. Keep the successful product outcome equivalent.", "state": {"activity": "done", "change": "active"}}, {"label": "Repeat", "caption": "Compare the same scenario and record uncertainty. A lower battery percentage in one run is insufficient attribution evidence.", "state": {"change": "done", "compare": "active"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence. Use Previous, Next or Play; every step is also available as text.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Power Budgets :: staff -->

Tie the budget to delivered user value, including freshness and reliability. Avoid “saving power” merely by dropping required work.

<!-- @before Further Reading :: check -->

### Check your diagnosis

<details><summary>Does Dispatchers.IO itself prove lower energy?</summary>

No. It changes execution placement; work amount and hardware activity still need measurement.

</details>

<details><summary>Why check tool support before collecting?</summary>

Power rails and related signals vary by device and platform. An absent signal is not a measured zero.

</details>

<details><summary>What should stay equal in a power comparison?</summary>

The useful outcome, duration and relevant device/network/app conditions.

</details>

<!-- @before Further Reading :: deeper -->

Primary evidence for the added layer: [Power Profiler](https://developer.android.com/studio/profile/power-profiler), [Batterystats workflow](https://developer.android.com/topic/performance/power/setup-battery-historian).
